#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook for neo-dev-toolkit plugin.
# Injects a reminder so Claude proactively invokes the neo-dev-toolkit skills
# (neo, ingest, api-spec, openapi-doc, open-collection, confluence-api-doc, gitlab,
#  atlassian, init-project, migrate-project, e2e-playwright) whenever a user request matches their
# triggering contexts. neo delegates the SDLC to using-agent-skills, which ships in
# the separately-installed upstream agent-skills plugin (a prerequisite, not bundled here).

set -euo pipefail

# Escape a string for safe embedding inside a JSON string literal.
# Uses bash parameter substitution for speed (single C-level pass per rule).
escape_for_json() {
    local s="$1"
    s="${s//\\/\\\\}"
    s="${s//\"/\\\"}"
    s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"
    s="${s//$'\r'/\\r}"
    s="${s//$'\t'/\\t}"
    printf '%s' "$s"
}

session_context="<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>
You have the neo-dev-toolkit plugin installed. It ships **neo** — a customized \`using-agent-skills\` meta-skill wrapped in loop engineering — an **ingest** memory primitive, and your project-specific connectors. Invoke them via the Skill tool whenever the user's request matches. neo forks the upstream discovery router and points it at the lifecycle skills in the **separately-installed upstream agent-skills plugin** — a prerequisite (see below).

## agent-skills lifecycle — the lifecycle skills neo routes to (external prerequisite)

neo's \`SKILL.md\` **forks** the \`using-agent-skills\` router — skill discovery (its flowchart), the 6 Core Operating Behaviors (surface assumptions, manage confusion, push back, enforce simplicity, maintain scope, verify), the spec→ship Lifecycle Sequence, and the Skill Rules — and **wraps it in a loop**. But the lifecycle SKILLS that router points to (\`spec-driven-development\`, \`test-driven-development\`, …), plus the specialist agents and slash commands, ship in the **upstream agent-skills plugin (github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills), which must be installed separately**; this toolkit no longer bundles them. **neo embeds the customized router + the loop, and references the external lifecycle skills by name.** If the upstream plugin is not installed, the skills neo routes to are missing.

## neo toolkit

1. **\`neo\`** — The \`using-agent-skills\` meta-skill in a customized form, wrapped in loop engineering. neo forks the upstream discovery router (flowchart + 6 Core Operating Behaviors + spec→ship lifecycle) and drives it as a recursive goal: frame an OBSERVABLE exit condition, iterate the lifecycle, verify with a fresh-context maker-checker against evidence, and stop on one of four independent exits (verifier · iteration cap · budget · no-progress). **On every \`/neo\` the loop is MANDATORY: neo's first output is the framed goal + observable exit condition + a \`docs/tasks/<slug>/STATE.md\` file — it does NOT edit source before STATE.md exists, and it DRIVES the lifecycle itself in order: it never asks the user to pick a methodology (no \"vertical-slice vs build-all-at-once\" menus, and never an option that contradicts \`incremental-implementation\`). It records any skipped phase as a \`waiver:\` in STATE.md — waivable only for work that does NOT add or change behavior; a silent skip fails neo's process-integrity gate, and for feature work the spec/design artifact is non-waivable. A large feature (multiple AC / layers) gets one plan-approval checkpoint after Plan before Build; small tasks proceed without pausing. neo's only user-facing pauses are goal ambiguity, that checkpoint, and the commit/PR human gate.** neo owns only four things: (1) the loop, (2) project memory (\`docs/tasks/<slug>/STATE.md\` + \`docs/knowledge/\`), (3) the exit condition (which AUGMENTS Behavior #6 \"Verify, Don't Assume\", never replaces it), and (4) a human gate at commit/PR. Three local additions sit in the router: \`ingest\` (Define — \"have a context, need knowledge\"), \`api-spec\` (Ship — \"update api-spec?\"), and \`e2e-playwright\` (Verify — HTTP e2e per acceptance criterion). The lifecycle skills the router points to are the external agent-skills prerequisite; neo references them by name and never re-derives the SDLC. Triggers: \"/neo\", \"neo\", a JIRA card id or URL, a GitLab MR URL, or any task that benefits from a recursive-goal loop with durable memory and a human gate. Resume with \"/neo continue <slug>\". NOT for single-file fixes, quick questions, or pure research — answer those directly; for standalone skill work use \`/spec\`, \`/test\`, etc.

2. **\`ingest\`** — Standalone memory primitive: ingest an external source (JIRA card, Confluence page, URL, image, HTML, text, verbal) ONCE into \`docs/knowledge/<topic>.md\` as curated, reusable context with provenance (source url, fetched_at, version/etag). Callable directly as \"/ingest <url>\" to pre-warm the knowledge base, or neo triggers it during the loop's ingest-first step. Maintains \`docs/knowledge/INDEX.md\`. Triggers: \"/ingest\", \"ingest <source>\", \"remember this\", \"add <url> to the knowledge base\".

3. **\`gitlab\`** — Low-level GitLab \`glab\` execution arm (a connector): read/summarize an MR, update MR description, list MRs, view MR/CI status, fetch CI logs, approve MRs, open/advance an MR. neo's human gate invokes it at commit/PR. Triggers: a bare GitLab MR URL, \"อ่าน MR\", \"ดู MR\", \"อัพเดท MR\", \"list MRs\", \"check pipeline\", \"approve MR\".

4. **\`atlassian\`** — Drive Jira + Confluence from the terminal via the \`acli\` CLI (a connector): view/search/create/edit/transition/assign Jira work items, manage sprints/boards/projects, read Confluence pages + manage spaces. A thin shell over \`acli --help\` that carries the command map + JQL/workflow/safety judgment. Triggers: \"ดู issue ของฉัน\", \"view my issues\", \"transition ไป In Progress\", \"สร้าง bug ใน Jira\", \"search ด้วย JQL\", \"ดู Confluence page\", \"list spaces\", or any raw acli op.

5. **\`api-spec\`** — Author the custom-YAML api-spec at \`docs/api/*.yaml\` — the spec-first source of truth and the head of the api-doc chain: \`_meta.yaml\` + one \`<domain>/<endpoint>.yaml\` per endpoint (method/path/auth, request/response field tables with M/O + Remark, multi-flow business_logic, errors, optional covers_ac). Not OpenAPI. Three-layer verify (apispeccheck.py + fresh-eyes + completeness). Triggers: \"author api spec\", \"create the api contract\", \"gen api spec\", \"สร้าง api spec\", \"เขียน docs/api\", \"update api spec\", \"validate api spec\".

6. **\`openapi-doc\`** — Verify Go source against the custom-YAML api-spec at \`docs/api/*.yaml\` and emit a drift report (added/removed routes, field presence, M/O, type mismatches). Writes nothing (report-only). Triggers: \"check go against api-spec\", \"api drift report\", \"เช็ค code ตรงกับ api-spec ไหม\", \"หา drift api\".

7. **\`open-collection\`** — Generate a runnable, self-documenting Bruno OpenCollection FROM the \`docs/api/*.yaml\` api-spec (Spec mode or AC-scenario mode). Update/validate too. Triggers: \"gen open collection\", \"สร้าง open collection\", \"สร้าง bruno จาก api spec\".

8. **\`confluence-api-doc\`** — Publish API docs to Confluence from the \`docs/api/*.yaml\` api-spec (one endpoint = one page under domain parents, via acli + REST). Triggers: \"publish api doc\", \"sync api doc\", \"push doc to confluence\", \"อัปเดต api doc ไป confluence\".

9. **\`init-project\`** — Scaffold a brand-new Go hexagonal/DDD microservice from a bundled frozen template (clean layers + tooling + infra + \`.kiro/\` + CLAUDE.md, zero domains). Builds + serves \`GET /health\` immediately. Triggers: \"init project\", \"/init-project\", \"scaffold a service\", \"สร้าง service ใหม่\", \"โครง service เปล่า\".

10. **\`migrate-project\`** — Refactor an EXISTING Go service onto the hexagonal/DDD blueprint — the brownfield sibling of init-project. Plans as ordered verifiable slices, executes with \`git mv\` (history-preserving), verifies each. Triggers: \"migrate project\", \"/migrate-project\", \"refactor to hexagonal/clean architecture\", \"ย้ายโครงสร้างโปรเจกต์\".

11. **\`e2e-playwright\`** — Author + **run** HTTP end-to-end tests, one per acceptance criterion, for a service with a Jest + Playwright-\`request\` harness (\`tests/e2e/specs/*.e2e.ts\`; the Jest runner driving Playwright's HTTP client, NOT the \`@playwright/test\` runner). Titles each test \`[<CARD> - AC-NNN] <desc> → <expected>\` so every AC is traceable; discovers the project's e2e layout, authors from the card's ACs + the api-spec contract, brings the service up if needed, runs the suite, and maps pass/fail back to each AC. Three-layer verify (e2echeck.py coverage tripwire + fresh-eyes + completeness). neo gates HTTP work on this via the \`e2e-ac\` exit criterion (only HTTP-observable ACs count; a non-observable AC is a declared \`it.skip\` with a reason). Triggers: \"write e2e\", \"gen e2e from AC\", \"run e2e\", \"e2e playwright\", \"เขียน e2e\", \"รัน e2e\", \"e2e ตาม AC\". Also when neo delegates the HTTP/API e2e Verify step.

Single-file fixes, quick questions, and pure research tasks do NOT need these skills — answer directly.
</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"

context_escaped=$(escape_for_json "$session_context")

# Emit the Claude Code hook output shape.
printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$context_escaped"

exit 0
